<PublicationLayout
  category="Engineering"
  type="Upstream report"
  title="An extreme-tail sign error in SciPy’s Student-t quantile"
  description="A reproducible wrong-sign and wrong-finiteness result in an extreme tail, reported to SciPy and routed upstream to Boost."
  published="August 23, 2026"
  status="Open upstream issue — no fix accepted or released"
>
  <section>
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Reported to SciPy on August 20, 2026. The issue remains open and is labeled
        <code>defect</code>, <code>scipy.special</code>, and <code>upstream bug</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A SciPy contributor requested an upstream Boost report. No fix has been accepted
        or released.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What we observed</h2>
    <p>
      For very small tail probabilities, <code>scipy.special.stdtrit</code> and
      <code>scipy.stats.t.ppf</code> can return positive infinity where the corresponding
      Student-t quantile is a large negative finite number. The report includes a
      reproducible binary64 input and an independent high-precision calculation for the
      expected value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For one reported case with three degrees of freedom, the returned value is
      <code>+inf</code>, while the expected result is approximately
      <code>-2.31e82</code>. The problem therefore changes both sign and finiteness rather
      than only the last few digits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>
      The observed failures occur at exceptionally small probabilities, with onset that
      depends on the degrees of freedom. This report does not claim that ordinary
      Student-t analyses are affected, nor does it estimate how often real research
      workflows reach the failing region.
    </p>
    <p>
      A SciPy contributor identified the inverse incomplete-beta path in Boost as the
      likely upstream location and left the repair decision to the Boost maintainers.
      That upstream investigation is not yet complete.
    </p>
  </section>

